  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 844 PM MDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nMonsoon moisture is slipping away, as high pressure expands west \nfrom the Southern Plains. Our lower and mid levels are still fairly \nmoist with dewpoints down, but still in the 50s, and our PWAT is \nstill holding on at 1.3\", but our upper levels have really begun to \ndry out, and that alone,has brought about a surprisingly big drop in \naerial coverage of showers and storms. In addition, the lack of \nwidespread thick afternoon cloud development has, and will, result \nin warmer temperatures. These two trends, drying and warming will \ncontinue through the remainder of the work week, as the high \npressure continues to build and become the dominant weather factor \nacross the Borderland. Thus, Thursday and Friday look \"mostly\" dry \nacross the area, with a good probability that only the area \nmountains see isolated shower/storm activity, with only slight \nchances for any location across the lowlands. We could also begin to \nsee some 100 degree max temperature readings across the lowlands \nThursday, and certainly Friday.\n\nBy Saturday and Sunday, some of the deeper moisture that was in \nplace over our region in days past, will have wrapped around the \nupper high, on it's track north through AZ, across the Four Corners, \neast across Colorado, and then back south into New Mexico, to \ncomplete a rotation around the high, landing back over our region. \nThis second iteration, of now recycled moisture won't be near as \nabundant as it's first passing, but we should see some increasing \nrain/storm chances return, especially over area mountains for the \nweekend. However, as the moisture is not as robust as last week, we \nwill still keep the temperatures hot, with lowland triples. \n\nFor next week, the GFS and ECMWF models keep the ridge near us, but \nthey both recenter it over the Southern Plains. This should \nconcentrate the moisture over AZ and W NM, with a focus of storms \nover our western zones, and predominately warm/hot and dry \nconditions over areas near and east of the Rio Grande Valley.",
